WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.012 --> 00:00:03.470
PROFESSOR WILDER:
My mother's dream

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.290
was for us to go to college.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.370
And I think for a lot of
first-generation college

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.330
students, college
represents this opportunity,

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.730
not just for us, but
for our whole families

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:16.790
to change the story
of their lives.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.330
JIMMY ROJAS: My
name is Jimmy Rojas.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.520
I am from Costa Rica.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.779
I am an international
student here at MIT.

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:23.820
WALTER MENENDEZ: My parents
didn't go to college.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.100
My mom's a housekeeper.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.480
My dad's a construction worker.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.500
Me getting into college
was a pretty big deal.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.880
CHRISTINE WALLEY: My
dad was a steel worker.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.810
He didn't graduate
from high school.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.366
My grandfather had a
grammar school education.

00:00:36.366 --> 00:00:37.490
He was also a steel worker.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.490
My great grandfather
was a steel worker.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.040
And I grew up in an area where
it wasn't really expected

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.512
that people were going
to go to college.

00:00:44.512 --> 00:00:47.350
JIMMY ROJAS: My parents, they
went to elementary school

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:48.670
and that's it.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.820
They were very supportive.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.010
And there were always,
like-- people tell you

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.700
that getting into
MIT isn't possible.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.250
But we think that you can do it.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.187
Because you're putting
a lot of effort.

00:01:01.187 --> 00:01:03.020
SAMANTHA LEWIS: I sort
of went through a lot

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:07.470
of the process on my own just
because my parents hadn't

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:08.970
gone through the
process themselves.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.320
So they didn't really--
they were there for me.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.280
But they couldn't
provide a lot of advice.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.930
WALTER MENENDEZ: Filling out
applications, taking tests,

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.940
sending in scores, and
getting recommendations--

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.740
it was a lot of things.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.770
It took a lot of figuring out.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.450
And a lot of it was just like,
I had to ask my teachers.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.710
I had to ask family friends.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.700
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Right when I
went away to this prep school,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.360
my dad was working
temporarily as a janitor.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.370
And when I said I wanted to go
away to this boarding school,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:39.581
he almost started to cry.

00:01:39.581 --> 00:01:41.080
And he said, I don't
want you to go.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.550
Because I'm afraid you're going
to come back and look down

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:44.909
at me because I'm a janitor.

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:46.950
WALTER MENENDEZ: There
was this very brief stint,

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.825
like around my sophomore
year of high school,

00:01:48.825 --> 00:01:52.020
where, for a split second it
was like, am I going to college?

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.020
Is that something I should do?

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:55.687
It was that bad.

00:01:55.687 --> 00:01:57.269
It was to the point
where it was like,

00:01:57.269 --> 00:01:59.235
I didn't know if I
should go to college.

00:01:59.235 --> 00:02:01.580
JIMMY ROJAS: After
all of that effort,

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.290
I ended up getting into MIT.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.200
Amazing.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.572
Just an unbelievable feeling.

00:02:09.572 --> 00:02:11.780
PROFESSOR WILDER: I had
always been a bright student.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.405
So to go to college
and all of a sudden

00:02:13.405 --> 00:02:16.730
find out that there were these
things I was supposed to know,

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.070
and that everybody
else in the classroom

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.410
had learned stuff that
I had never learned,

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.120
was a humbling and
demoralizing experience that

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.750
led me at times to just
retreat and think to myself,

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.080
this is not the place for me.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.150
DIEGO GIRALDEZ: Your
family is typically

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.120
almost counting on you.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.203
You've made them so proud.

00:02:35.203 --> 00:02:36.470
It's such a rare thing, maybe.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:37.710
You're the first one to
go to college, first one

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:38.626
to try this thing out.

00:02:38.626 --> 00:02:40.650
And particularly at
a place like MIT,

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.485
it's, you know-- you don't
want to disappoint anyone.

00:02:43.485 --> 00:02:45.610
PROFESSOR WILDER: But the
last thing I wanted to do

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.984
was walk into a
professor's office and say,

00:02:48.984 --> 00:02:50.860
I'm not following something.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.420
I don't know what's happening.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.510
I need help with such and such.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.350
It took me, I think, until
the very end of college

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.995
to work up that kind of courage.

00:02:58.995 --> 00:03:01.850
SAMANTHA LEWIS:
For me, college is

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:03.760
the make it or break it thing.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.000
It's not-- there is no
option to change my mind.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.010
PROFESSOR WILDER: My
mother witnessed her dream

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.050
come true-- her two kids
heading off to college.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:20.060
And I think she simultaneously
worried and was dreadfully

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:23.490
afraid that college
was going to change us.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.490
JIMMY ROJAS: I feel like
I belong here after all.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:32.960
I was here for just six months,
and then I went to Costa Rica.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.054
But I wanted to come back.

00:03:34.054 --> 00:03:36.220
PROFESSOR WILDER: When we
came home on the holidays,

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.580
we were under great scrutiny.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.520
Everyone was looking at how
we talked, and how we sat,

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.260
and what we ate,
and how we behaved,

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.310
and what music we listened to.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.386
They had to figure out
what was happening to us--

00:03:49.386 --> 00:03:50.760
whether or not we
still belonged.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.690
CHRISTINE WALLEY: When
students move away

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:56.420
to go to elite schools,
there is a real rupture

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.460
that gets created
with families at home.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.300
Their families are coming
from different backgrounds.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.020
And oftentimes I think both
the students and the parents

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.150
don't really know
how to address that.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.510
 

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:13.225
And those feelings get
buried a lot, I think.

00:04:13.225 --> 00:04:16.670
SAMANTHA LEWIS: I am my family's
support system once I graduate.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:18.360
JIMMY ROJAS: My
mother has always

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.540
wanted to be in an airplane.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.950
My father has always
wanted to have a telescope.

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:26.110
In a few years,
I think I will be

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.920
able to make those dreams real.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:32.690
I put a lot of effort
to change my life.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:36.550
And I would like to do
something to change theirs.

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.750
Not just my parents, also
my sister, and hopefully

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.010
everyone else in my family.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:45.389
And hopefully everyone
else in the country.

00:04:45.389 --> 00:04:47.430
PROFESSOR WILDER: The very
success of our careers

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.110
has made us sort of invisible.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.190
And I walk into room
now, and people see me

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:55.360
as the head of the
history faculty.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.790
But they don't see me as a guy
from Bedford Stuyvesant who

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.430
had a good chance of not even
making it through college.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:06.280
JIMMY ROJAS: I now have
a much bigger perspective

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:07.590
of the world.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.670
And I understand many things
that I didn't understand.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.840
I was living in like a bubble.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.970
And I had no idea what was
happening outside that place.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.865
So MIT has opened my mind.

00:05:20.865 --> 00:05:22.460
SAMANTHA LEWIS:
It's life changing.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.590
Because it just opens
up a couple other world

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.790
of opportunities than I would
have ever had access to.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:31.930
And I feel like an MIT degree,
no matter what you study,

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:33.874
there are lots of
ways to apply it.

00:05:33.874 --> 00:05:35.290
PRESIDENT REIF: I
want to conclude

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:40.960
by celebrating and thanking
the good people of this world,

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.110
while at the same
time honoring a most

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.020
important couple in my life.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.290
The couple in my story
left Eastern Europe

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:52.240
in the late 1930s during
one of the darkest periods

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.010
in its history and found
refuge in South America.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.970
This couple raised four sons
under extremely difficult

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:03.810
circumstances, but raised
them with principles,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:06.220
with integrity and values.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.170
Out of the goodness
of good people,

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.885
this couple escaped
direct catastrophe.

00:06:10.885 --> 00:06:15.450
So today I want to honor
everyone who's struggling

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:19.720
and who dreams of a brighter
future for their children,

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.480
and to tell each of
them that there is hope.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.770
Because the youngest son
of the couple in my story

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:29.630
eventually became the
17th president of one

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:32.550
of the most remarkable
educational institutions

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.350
the world has ever seen.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:38.499
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:38.499 --> 00:06:42.070
JIMMY ROJAS: Many people have
dreams like the one I had.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:44.790
They feel like those
dreams are basically

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:46.910
impossible to achieve.

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:49.970
So with an MIT degree,
with a college degree,

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:54.640
I think I will the voice to show
the world that you can actually

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:55.840
achieve your dreams.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:22.561
 

